    "title": "Medical examiner testifies in Brandy Dowdy case - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Taylor Mitchell Emily Moessner ,\n2 hours ago\nFRANKLIN COUNTY, Ala. (WHNT) — Testimony continued Wednesday in the case of what happened to two women attacked by a pack of dogs in Franklin County.\nProsecutors say those dogs belonged to Brandy Dowdy.\nJury seated in trial of Red Bay woman accused in deadly dog attacks On Wednesday, the jury was shown a series of often graphic autopsy photos of one of the victims, Summer Beard.\nThose photos went alongside testimony from a state medical examiner, Dr. Valerie Green.\nGreen testified that she found the cause of death for Summer Beard to be from injuries caused by a K-9.\nThe medical examiner testified that there were “too many puncture wounds to count” on Beard’s body.\n📲 Download the WHNT News 19 App to stay updated on the go.\n📧 Sign up for WHNT News 19 newsletters to have news sent to your inbox.\nBeard was an Alabama Department of Public Health employee who went to a property on Crumpton Road in Red Bay in April of 2022, to investigate one day after the first victim, Michelle Sheeks, was attacked.\nDuring Wednesday’s testimony, a nurse practitioner who treated Sheeks at a hospital in Mississippi after her attack testified.\nHe testified that Sheeks’ death was a result of “dog bites”.\nAn investigator with the Franklin County Sheriff’s Office also testified Wednesday afternoon in Beard’s death.\nHe said when he arrived on the scene, there was a pack of “6 to 9” dogs that were being aggressive.\nHe testified that he saw the dogs bite one of the people who was on scene, and that he believes it was the same “pack of dogs” that attacked Summer Beard.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WHNT.com.",
